  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 352 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nQuite the change from the record-breaking warmth over the\nweekend as much cooler Canadian air continues to settle over \nthe Upper Midwest. A few rain showers have developed across\nwestern Minnesota where clearer skies lead to some surface\ninstability in addition to the cold cyclonic flow aloft. These \nshowers will continue into central & eastern Minnesota until \nsunset, & a rumble or two of thunder can't be ruled out. The \ncooler temperatures continue through the rest of the week as \ncontinued northerly flow & mostly cloudy skies keep \ntemperatures mainly in the 50s to low 60s.\n\nOur next chance for rain arrives Thursday as an upper-level low\nmoves northeast out of the Rockies into the central Plains. \nRain should spread into southern Minnesota & western Wisconsin \nduring the morning and continue through the afternoon & evening.\nMost of the moisture with this system will remain south of the\narea across the mid-Mississippi valley along with the heaviest \nrainfall across Iowa & southern Minnesota. rainfall amounts \nwill generally range around 0.25-0.50\" across southern \nMinnesota & western Wisconsin & around 0.1\" or less farther \nnorth. \n\nA colder airmass moves into the region behind Thursdays system \nas a strong upper-level trough digs into the Great Lakes & cold \nCanadian high pressure moves over the Upper Midwest. Gusty \nnorthwest winds & highs only in the low to mid 50s are expected \nFriday before high pressure brings clearing skies & diminishing \nwinds Friday night. This sets the stage for widespread freezing \ntemperatures Saturday morning, with lows generally in the upper \n20s to low 30s across much of central & southern Minnesota and \nwestern Wisconsin. AI weather models continue to run several \ndegrees colder than the traditional physical models & ensembles \nfor Saturday morning, which adds confidence to the potential for\na widespread freeze based on observed trends from last fall. \nThe extent of the colder temperatures will depend on how quickly\nwinds diminish Friday night, but this is increasingly looking \nlike a growing season ending freeze for much of the area.\n\nThe cold spell will be brief as upper-level ridging builds back\ninto the Upper Midwest over the weekend. Temperatures rebound\ninto the upper 50s to low 60s this weekend, followed by \nwidespread 60s & potentially low 70s early next week. Dry\nweather looks to continue into next week with only a low chance\nat showers & thunderstorms depending on the timing of a frontal\npassage Sunday into Monday.",
